{
  "dom_id": "step-e7425",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Points of Intersection of a Hyperbola and a Line",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095491/assets/c8d69cebc1c7388bb9bc8ac73152a9b2941156188917ee6b680f6758a3d777de.png)\n\nCalculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\dfrac{x^2}{9} - \\dfrac{y^2}{25}=1$ intersects the line $y = x.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16{x}^{2}}{225} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{225}{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
